\section{Begin with a Performance Question}

Michael Abrash uses the Zen Timer in \emph{Zen of Assembly Language} to argue for measuring rather than guessing. The timer itself belongs to its historical platform; the principle remains useful. A backend change can make a dependency chain shorter, reduce the steady-state cost of independent work, or reduce whole-program time. These are different claims. To test a scheduling-table entry, isolate the proposed bottleneck. To assess a real selector change, compare whole kernels and workloads as well, under the same input and compiler configuration. Check the emitted code first: an optimizing compiler may remove the computation, duplicate it, vectorize a baseline you intended to keep scalar, or place the measured loop differently in memory.

Intel's \emph{Optimization Reference Manual} distinguishes model-specific behavior, latency, throughput, and performance monitoring. Agner Fog's \emph{The Microarchitecture of Intel, AMD, and VIA CPUs} uses timings and micro-operation counts to infer processor behavior and discusses limitations of those inferences. An architectural instruction count is not a cycle model. The instruction stream consumes front-end, issue, and execution resources that differ between implementations; the same opcode can also have different timing for different input-to-output dependencies or operand forms.

\section{Worked Pair: Dependence and Throughput}

To learn about a register-register integer add, compare two small loops on the same core. Keep input constants ready in registers, and use an identical loop branch and number of adds per iteration. The first loop applies all adds to one accumulator. Each add reads the previous result, so its useful work lies on one loop-carried dependency chain. The second loop distributes the same number of adds evenly across four accumulators, allowing overlap between chains. Unroll both loops enough to keep the loop branch from dominating, and consume all final accumulator values outside the measured interval. If implemented in a higher-level language, inspect the output to verify that the compiler did not replace repeated modular additions with a closed form.

Count operations explicitly: eight adds per iteration for $10^7$ iterations means $8\times10^7$ adds. If the first loop has a steady-state slope of $8.0$ core cycles per iteration and the second $2.4$ in an illustrative run, the measured costs are $1.0$ and $0.30$ cycles per add respectively. These are calculations from hypothetical observations, not timing data for a specific core. The second loop would sustain about $3.3$ adds per core cycle; this does not establish the core's peak add rate. Increase the number of independent chains and vary the unroll and alignment. A rate that keeps changing is evidence that the original loop was not isolating a stable limit.

\section{Measure the Setup as Well as the Code}

Record processor model and core type, affinity, frequency policy, toolchain, exact instruction bytes, inputs, and whether the run targets cold or warm caches. On a heterogeneous desktop CPU, pinning prevents a thread from alternating between different execution engines. A reference timestamp counter and a core cycle counter need not advance at the same rate; label the units, and use a timing boundary appropriate to the mechanism so unrelated out-of-order work does not contaminate a short interval. Sweep the repetition count to separate fixed setup costs from per-iteration cost rather than subtracting a single empty-loop time mechanically. A competing process, interrupt, migration, or frequency change may remain visible even with a good harness.

\section{Use Counters to Test Explanations}

Collect a distribution across repeated measurements and alternate the order of variants to limit drift. If retired instructions fall but cycles rise, test one explanation at a time: dependency pressure, data misses, or a fetch effect of changed alignment. Consult event definitions for the tested processor; event names can conceal different scopes, and multiplexed events can be particularly noisy in short runs. Validate outputs before interpreting timing changes, and carry a case where the optimization should not help. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/31-causal-profiling-ir.md}{Causal Profiling IR} proposes tracking such hypotheses explicitly. Send narrow evidence back to the cost table in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-225}; a single microbenchmark should not override measurements of the target workloads.
